#!args scenario=Scenario_AllCards seed=42
#
# A Response card is secret until it is activated, and secret again once it returns to play.
#
# Response cards are played FACE DOWN onto the table and only flipped when used as a reaction, so a
# player who does not control the card's faction must see a card back instead of its face. That is a
# per-peer render decision made by CardFace.ForCard via CardState.IsFaceVisibleToLocalPlayer, which
# no headless run can exercise — but the replicated half of it, CardState.IsRevealed, is exactly what
# this test pins.
#
# `cardrevealed` reads that raw flag. It is deliberately NOT the same question as "has this card ever
# been activated": ActivatedInTurns is permanent (it gates CardData.MultipleActivationsPerTurn and
# Condition.CardHasNotBeenActivatedThisTurn, so it must never be cleared), while a spent Response card
# recycled back into a deck, a hand, or onto the table has to go face down again. Deriving visibility
# from ActivatedInTurns instead would leave a recycled card revealed forever.
#
# The flag is meaningful only for Response cards — every other card type is public regardless of it,
# which is why nothing here asserts on a Status card.
#
# Scenario_AllCards starts every Response card on the table, so no setup deals one. Destroyers ("use
# when a supplied US or UK Navy is about to be removed") is the one Response card an existing test
# already drives a block window for — see block_reaction_prevents_removal.qgc, which shares this
# removal setup.
#
# Values below are pinned to the seed in the #!args line above. Unit 110 is the Navy built by the
# force deployUnit line, not a scenario deployment; re-derive it if unit id allocation changes.
# Run:  tests/run-cli.sh tests/qgc/response_card_reveal.qgc

prompt                                  # turn 1, Germany, PLAY_CARD — synchronise with the game

# On the table from the scenario, never activated: face down for everyone but the UK.
assert card ResponseDestroyers in response
assert cardrevealed ResponseDestroyers == false

# The US only starts with an Army in US East. Give it a Navy in the adjacent sea so Destroyers'
# "supplied Navy" trigger can match. `force` (IsTrigger=false) keeps the setup out of the reaction
# chain.
force deployUnit --faction UNITED_STATES --country NORTH_ATLANTIC --deploy BUILD

# Removing that Navy opens the UK's block window and offers Destroyers.
api removeUnit --faction JAPAN --unit 110 --reason BATTLE
play 1                                  # UK activates Destroyers

# Activating Destroyers opens its own block window and then its activation window, and every faction
# holding a face-down Response card is asked in both whether or not it can react — that is the
# always-ask rule (CardPlayRound.ShouldOpenReactionWindow), and it is what stops the prompt itself
# from revealing what the hidden card does. Scenario_AllCards puts Response cards on every table, so
# these seven windows all open with nothing on offer. Explicit passes rather than `auto 7`: if the
# set ever changes, the mismatch should surface here rather than be absorbed silently.
pass                                    # block window on the activation: Japan   (no options)
pass                                    #                                Italy    (no options)
pass                                    #                                Soviet   (no options)
pass                                    # activation window:             Japan    (no options)
pass                                    #                                Italy    (no options)
pass                                    #                                UK       (no options)
pass                                    #                                Soviet   (no options)

# Activated: flipped face up for every peer, and spent, so it moves to the discard pile.
# ActivateReactionChangeEvent sets the flag inside ExecuteAsync, which ChangeEvent.ApplyMutation
# awaits BEFORE it enqueues AfterAnimations — so the card is already revealed when the
# "UK activates Destroyers" modal renders it. FAILS (false) if that write is lost or moved later.
assert cardrevealed ResponseDestroyers == true
assert card ResponseDestroyers in discard

# Back to hand: face down again. FAILS (true) if the reveal is derived from ActivatedInTurns.
force recycleCard --faction UNITED_KINGDOM --card ResponseDestroyers --destination Hand
assert card ResponseDestroyers in hand
assert cardrevealed ResponseDestroyers == false

# Shuffled into the draw deck: still face down.
force recycleCard --faction UNITED_KINGDOM --card ResponseDestroyers --destination ShuffleIntoDeck
assert card ResponseDestroyers in deck
assert cardrevealed ResponseDestroyers == false

# Top of the draw deck: still face down.
force recycleCard --faction UNITED_KINGDOM --card ResponseDestroyers --destination TopOfDeck
assert card ResponseDestroyers in deck
assert cardrevealed ResponseDestroyers == false

# Back onto the table: a replayed Response card is face down like any other. DeckState.PlayCard
# clears the flag on the RESPONSE branch, so this holds even if a future caller puts a revealed card
# straight onto the table without recycling it through a hand or deck first.
force playCard --card ResponseDestroyers
assert card ResponseDestroyers in response
assert cardrevealed ResponseDestroyers == false

# A Response card that was never activated is untouched by any of the above.
assert cardrevealed ResponseRAF == false
assert card ResponseRAF in response

quit
